--TEST--
`Core\Command::run` answers a command line its table cannot fill with a usage status, and runs no handler — `rule:tooling/commands-are-compiled`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// A command line is *input*, so nothing about one is a throw: § 6's answer to a
// word the table has no row for is a usage error — the generated page to
// standard error, and a status the caller decides what to do with. The two
// halves worth pinning are that the status comes back at all, and that the
// handler did not run: a matcher that called it with a missing argument would
// print the first line below either way.
class Greeter {
    #[Core\Command(name: "greet", about: "Greet somebody by name")]
    public static function greet(string $name, #[Core\Option] bool $loud = false): uint {
        echo "greet ran, and nothing on this command line selected it\n";
        return 0;
    }
}

// `greet` is spelled right and its one positional is missing, which is the
// failure a parser is most likely to answer with a plausible empty string.
echo "status: ", Core\Command::run(), "\n";
echo "still running", "\n";
--ARGS--
greet
--EXPECT--
status: 2
still running
